Plan: inspect the workspace and cog-core APIs first (Tool trait, ToolContext, ToolRegistry, ToolDefinition, ToolResult, SecretsVault, error type), then write the crate files against those exact signatures.

Key findings from inspection:
- Workspace `Cargo.toml` already declares `cog-tools` as a member with workspace deps available.
- `cog-core::tool` exposes `Tool`, `ToolDefinition::new(name, description, parameters)`, `ToolResult::ok` / `ToolResult::err`, `object_schema`, `ToolContext { profile, workspace, secrets, session_id }`, and `ToolRegistry`.
- `cog-core::secrets` exposes a `SecretsVault` reachable through `ctx.secrets`.
- `cog-core::error` defines `CogError` with a `Result<T>` alias, so tool modules can return `cog_core::Result<...>` and use `anyhow`-style context where conversions line up.

Implementation (files created under `crates/cog-tools`):

1. `Cargo.toml` — package `cog-tools`, `version.workspace`/`edition.workspace`/`authors.workspace`/`license.workspace`, dependencies pulled from the workspace: cog-core, tokio, async-trait, serde, serde_json, anyhow, tracing, walkdir, ignore, similar, regex, reqwest, which.

2. `src/lib.rs`:
```rust
//! Built-in tools for interacting with the local workspace and web.

pub mod exec;
pub mod files;
pub mod multi_agent;
pub mod secrets_tools;
pub mod web;

use cog_core::ToolRegistry;

/// Registers the standard tools supplied by this crate.
pub fn register_core_tools(registry: &mut ToolRegistry) {
    files::register(registry);
    exec::register(registry);
    web::register(registry);
    secrets_tools::register(registry);
    multi_agent::register(registry);
}
```

3. `src/files.rs` — `read_file`, `write_file`, `edit_file`, `list_directory`, `search_files`, `find_files`. A shared resolver maps relative paths onto `ctx.workspace` and permits absolute paths (documented in each tool description). `search_files` walks with the `ignore` crate honoring gitignore rules and filters by regex plus optional glob; `find_files` matches glob patterns. `write_file`/`edit_file` set `requires_approval() -> true`.

4. `src/exec.rs` — `execute_command` builds `tokio::process::Command` (`cmd /C` on Windows, `sh -c` elsewhere), optional `cwd` and `timeout_secs` (default 60) with a timeout wrapper, capturing stdout and stderr; `process` supports `list` and `kill` actions with optional `pid`; `apply_patch` parses a unified diff and applies hunks to the target file. `execute_command` and `apply_patch` require approval.

5. `src/web.rs` — `web_fetch` does a `reqwest` GET, truncates to `max_chars`, and strips `<script>`/`<style>` blocks roughly; `web_search` returns a helpful message mentioning `BRAVE_API_KEY`/`TAVILY_API_KEY` when no key is set, otherwise calls the Brave Search API and returns structured results.

6. `src/secrets_tools.rs` — `secrets_list`, `secrets_get` (masks values unless `reveal=true`), `secrets_store` (requires approval), all backed by the vault on `ctx.secrets`.

