## Verdict

Posttrain intentionally groups by **HF base `modelId`**, not by trained output. Every Gemma-4 4B SFT/RLHF project (and every library artifact linked to those projects) lands on **one card** titled something like `google/gemma-4-E4B-it`, with chips like `Library ×N` / `SFT ×N`. That's exactly the reported bug.

### 1. How keys are derived

`normalizeModelKey` only trims; empty becomes `__unassigned__`:

```js
// renderer/v120.js
function normalizeModelKey(id) {
  const s = String(id || '').trim();
  return s || POSTTRAIN_UNASSIGNED;
}
```

`refreshPosttrainGrid` buckets like this:

| Source | Key used |
|--------|----------|
| Library entry **with** `projectId` | `project.modelId` (fallback `m.name`) |
| Library entry **without** `projectId` | `m.name` (export folder basename) |
| SFT/RLHF project | `p.modelId` |

```js
// renderer/v120.js (refreshPosttrainGrid)
for (const m of libraryModels) {
  let key = POSTTRAIN_UNASSIGNED;
  if (m.projectId && projectById.has(m.projectId)) {
    key = normalizeModelKey(projectById.get(m.projectId).modelId || m.name);
  } else if (m.name) {
    key = normalizeModelKey(m.name);
  }
  ensure(key).library.push(m);
}

for (const p of state.projects || []) {
  if (p.type !== 'sft' && p.type !== 'rlhf') continue;
  const bucket = ensure(p.modelId);
  if (p.type === 'sft') bucket.sft.push(p);
  else bucket.rlhf.push(p);
}
```

Card title = that key (`entry.label`). Distinct train runs share one card whenever they share a base id.

### 2. What `library.list` returns

```js
// engine/bin/modelstudio-engine.js — 'library.list'
models.push({ name, path: full, hasAdapter, hasMerged });   // exportRoot dirs
// plus for each project with an existing modelPath:
models.push({
  name: p.name,          // project display name
  path: p.modelPath,     // unique output path
  projectId: p.id,
  hasAdapter, hasMerged,
});
```

No `modelId` on library rows. The distinctness lives in `name` / `path` / `projectId` — and Posttrain **throws that away** when `projectId` exists, rekeying via `project.modelId`.

### 3. Why Gemma variants collapse

`engine/lib/projects.js` stores the base catalog id on the project:

```js
// one model only
modelId: partial.modelId || null,   // HF id for sft base, or output name
modelPath: partial.modelPath || null, // local output path after train
```

So:
- Project A: `modelId = google/gemma-4-E4B-it`, `name = "Gemma customer support"`, `modelPath = .../output/gemma-customer-support`
- Project B: same `modelId`, different name/path
- Library rows for both get remapped to `google/gemma-4-E4B-it`
- Both projects land in the same bucket

→ **one card**, `Library ×2`, `SFT ×2`, etc. Matches "all my Gemma 4 4b models in one folder."

Orphan export folders (no `projectId`) still key by folder `name`, so they can appear as separate cards unless names collide.

### 4. Recommended grouping key

| Goal | Key | Label |
|------|-----|--------|
| **One card per trained model/output** (user intent) | `project.id` for project-backed rows; `m.path` for orphans | `project.name` / `m.name` |
| One card per base model (current) | `modelId` | HF id — causes this bug |
| Path-only uniqueness | `path.basename(modelPath)` / `m.path` | basename — fragile on name collisions |

**Best fix:** group Posttrain the way Model Library does ("one project = one model"):

- **Primary key:** `project.id` (SFT/RLHF cards + library rows with `projectId`)
- **Orphan exports:** `m.path` (stable) or `m.name`
- Show base `modelId` as subtitle/meta, not as the card identity

Avoid using library `name` alone as the key for project-backed outputs if you also keep project cards — the same project would duplicate unless merged by `projectId`.

Do **not** keep grouping by `modelId` if users expect separate cards per fine-tune.

### Posttrain UX note

- Grid shows model cards only; clicking opens the **action modal** (`#posttrain-action-modal`) with SFT / RLHF columns plus New SFT / New RLHF.
- There is no SFT/RLHF filter toggle on the Posttrain view header; the type split is modal-only.
- Model Library (`renderLibrary`) renders one card per **project** (by `p.name`), which is why that tab feels less "collapsed."

