## HIGH

### 1. Unbounded HTTP body allocation (memory exhaustion DoS)

**File:** `C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\server\src\http_api.cpp:271-280`

**Evidence:** Headers are capped at 16 MB (line 255), but `body_need` from `std::stoul` has no upper bound. The body read loop appends until `body_have >= body_need` with no size check:

```271:280:C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\server\src\http_api.cpp
  while (body_have < body_need) {
    // ... recv ...
    req.append(buf, (size_t)n);
    body_have += (size_t)n;
  }
```

A client can send `Content-Length: 2147483647` (or similar) and force unbounded `std::string` growth. Multiple concurrent requests can OOM the process.

**Note:** `std::stoul` exceptions are caught (lines 264-268), but valid large values are accepted.

---

### 2. Use-after-free on server shutdown (`ModelManager`)

**File:** `C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\server\src\http_api.cpp:352-356,516-528`  
**Also:** `C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\server\src\main.cpp:86-87`

**Evidence:** Client threads are detached and capture `ModelManager&` by reference:

```528:528:C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\server\src\http_api.cpp
    std::thread(handle_client, cs, std::ref(mgr), cfg).detach();
```

On `POST /shutdown`, `request_http_shutdown()` breaks the accept loop; `run_http_server()` returns immediately; `main()` destroys `mgr` while detached threads may still be in `read_request()`, `mgr.complete()`, or `mgr.load()`.

No join or shutdown barrier for in-flight client threads.

---

### 3. Use-after-free on engine exit (`JobManager`)

**File:** `C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\engine\src\job_manager.cpp:134-177`  
**Also:** `C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\engine\src\main.cpp:55,274-276,307-318`

**Evidence:** Job completion runs in a detached thread holding `JobManager* self`:

```134:148:C:\Users\Waylon Brown\Downloads\ModelStudio-App\engine\src\job_manager.cpp
  std::thread([self, id, proc]() {
    ProcessHandle ph = proc;
    int code = wait_process(ph);
    {
      std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(self->mu_);
      // ...
    }
    // ... accesses self->jobs_ again at 146-148
```

`g_jobs` is static. On stdin EOF or `shutdown` RPC (`std::exit(0)` at line 276), `JobManager` is destroyed while detached threads may still lock `self->mu_` and access `self->jobs_`.

