## Findings: why `NextAuth` is not callable

**Root cause:** an ambient module overwrite in `apps/web/src/types/next-auth.d.ts`.

### 1. `apps/web/tsconfig.json`
- `strict`, `esModuleInterop`, `moduleResolution: "bundler"`, `module: "esnext"` — all fine for next-auth v5.
- `include` covers `**/*.ts`, so `src/types/next-auth.d.ts` is loaded into the project.

### 2. Real package (`next-auth@5.0.0-beta.31`)
`node_modules/next-auth/index.d.ts` ends with a callable default export:

```ts
export default function NextAuth(config: NextAuthConfig | ...): NextAuthResult;
```

So `import NextAuth from "next-auth"` followed by `NextAuth({...})` is the documented, type-correct usage.

### 3. Local override (the bug)

```ts
declare module "next-auth" {
  interface Session {
    user: { id: string; /* ... */ };
  }
}
```

This file has no top-level `import`/`export`, so it is a **script/ambient declaration**. A bare `declare module "next-auth"` in an ambient context **replaces** the module instead of augmenting it. TypeScript then treats `"next-auth"` as only that `Session` interface — no default export, no call signatures.

### 4. Confirmed with project-mode `tsc`

```
src/lib/auth.ts(60,52): error TS2349: This expression is not callable.
  Type 'typeof import("next-auth")' has no call signatures.
```

Follow-on `implicitly has an 'any' type` errors on the callbacks are fallout from the same broken module shape.

Note: the ad-hoc `npx tsc ... src/lib/auth.ts` invocation did **not** surface TS2349 (no project context / `paths`, and the augmentation may not apply the same way). Project mode (`npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`) is the one that matters here.

### Fix
Make the file a real module augmentation by adding a top-level import:

```ts
import "next-auth";

declare module "next-auth" {
  interface Session {
    user: {
      id: string;
      name?: string | null;
      email?: string | null;
      image?: string | null;
      username?: string | null;
      balanceCents?: number;
    };
  }
}
```

With that import present, the file becomes a module, `declare module "next-auth"` is treated as augmentation (merging with the real types), and `NextAuth(...)` is callable again.

Plan: locate the installed next-auth package, read its entry type/runtime files, and check how NextAuth and NextAuthConfig are exported.

Key finding: `next-auth` is not under `apps/web/node_modules`; it is hoisted to the repo root (`node_modules/next-auth`). It is `next-auth@5.0.0-beta.31`.

Exact `NextAuth` signatures:

Types (`index.d.ts`) — default export only:
```ts
export default function NextAuth(
  config: NextAuthConfig | ((request: NextRequest | undefined) => Awaitable<NextAuthConfig>)
): NextAuthResult;
```

Runtime (`index.js`):
```js
export default function NextAuth(config) { ... }
```

There is **no named export** `NextAuth`. The correct import is:
```ts
import NextAuth from "next-auth"
```

Related exports from `"next-auth"`:
```ts
export { AuthError, CredentialsSignin } from "@auth/core/errors";
export { customFetch };
export type { Session, Account, Profile, DefaultSession, User } from "@auth/core/types";
export type { NextAuthConfig, NextAuthRequest };
export interface NextAuthResult { handlers; auth; signIn; signOut; unstable_update; }
```

`NextAuthConfig` — no broken type; it exists and is re-exported. Defined in `lib/index.d.ts`:
```ts
export interface NextAuthConfig extends Omit<AuthConfig, "raw"> {
  callbacks?: AuthConfig["callbacks"] & {
    authorized?: (params: {
      request: NextRequest;
      auth: Session | null;
    }) => Awaitable<boolean | NextResponse | Response | undefined>;
  };
}
```
Re-exported from the package root as `export type { NextAuthConfig, NextAuthRequest }`.

`package.json` main export:
```json
".": {
  "types": "./index.d.ts",
  "import": "./index.js",
  "default": "./index.js"
}
