**Short answer:** BrowserOS does **not** route your normal web browsing through their servers. Sites you visit see **your** public IP, the same way they would in Chrome or Firefox. BrowserOS is not a VPN or privacy proxy.

## What sites see

BrowserOS is a **local Chromium-based browser**. When you open `example.com`, the connection goes **directly from your computer** to that site. The site’s server sees your real public IP because that’s how HTTP/TCP works — there’s no built-in proxy or IP masking in the codebase.

This is different from **cloud browsers** (Perplexity Comet, ChatGPT Atlas, etc.), which can run browsing on remote infrastructure. BrowserOS explicitly positions itself as the opposite:

> *"Not Comet, Atlas, or Dia. Those AI browsers route your prompts through their cloud with their model. BrowserOS runs on your machine with your AI keys."*

## What BrowserOS itself sees

From their [privacy policy](https://browseros.com/privacy):

- They **do not** track browsing history, cookies, or URLs **without your consent**
- That data stays **on your machine** by default
- **Cloud sync is off by default** — if you turn it on, session data (history, cookies, URLs, etc.) is stored encrypted on their AWS servers for syncing across devices

So BrowserOS is **not** “handing your IP to sites” on your behalf. Sites get your IP because **you** connect to them directly.

## Other places your data can go

Separate from “sites seeing your IP”:

| Scenario | What leaves your machine |
|---|---|
| **Normal browsing** | Direct to websites (they see your IP) |
| **AI chat / agent** | Page content and prompts go to the AI provider you use (default Kimi/Moonshot, or BYOK, or local Ollama) |
| **Product telemetry** | Anonymous usage events (version, OS, tool usage) — BrowserClaw docs say **not** URLs or page content |
| **Cloud sync (opt-in)** | Browsing/session data to BrowserOS servers |

If you use the **built-in default AI model**, your prompts and page context are sent to that provider’s servers under **their** privacy terms. For maximum privacy, the docs recommend **BYOK** or **local models (Ollama)**, where AI traffic doesn’t need to leave your device.

## Bottom line

- **Sites:** They see your real IP — normal browser behavior, not BrowserOS-specific.
- **BrowserOS servers:** They don’t sit in the middle of your page loads.
- **Privacy focus:** Less about hiding your IP from websites, more about keeping browsing data local and letting you choose where AI data goes.

If you want sites **not** to see your home IP, you’d still need a VPN, Tor, or similar — BrowserOS doesn’t provide that out of the box.

