**No — BrowserOS does not have built-in parental controls** for blocking specific sites (e.g. “never visit x, y, z”).

I searched the docs, feature list, and codebase. There is no parental-controls feature, no site blocklist/allowlist UI, and no docs about restricting browsing for kids or managed profiles.

## What it does have (related, but not the same)

| Feature | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Ad blocking (uBlock Origin)** | Blocks ads/trackers via filter lists. You *can* add custom rules to block domains, but that’s extension config — not password-protected parental controls. |
| **Full internet access** | Docs explicitly say the agent can visit any website. There’s no built-in site restriction for the AI either. |
| **Chromium base** | Under the hood it’s Chromium, so *in theory* OS-level or enterprise-style policies *might* work, but BrowserOS doesn’t document or ship a parental-controls experience for this. |

## What you’d need instead

If you want “block sites x, y, z,” you’d use external tools:

1. **DNS filtering** — NextDNS, OpenDNS Family Shield, Pi-hole, etc. (network-wide)
2. **OS-level controls** — Windows Family Safety, macOS Screen Time, router parental controls
3. **uBlock Origin custom filters** — block specific domains in BrowserOS (user-managed, easy to bypass)
4. **Hosts file / firewall** — block domains at the machine level

## Bottom line

BrowserOS is built around **privacy and AI automation**, not **family/supervised browsing**. It does not offer Chrome-style Family Link or a first-party “blocked sites” list. For real parental controls, you’d layer network or OS controls on top of the browser.

